<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kogi State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kogi State is a state in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, created in 1991, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokoja">Lokoja</a> as its capital. It lies at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Benue rivers. Its peoples include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a>, Ebira and Okun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, and it contains the historic Igala capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idah">Idah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kogi-state.jpg" alt="Kogi State" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 7bdIa9 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kogi_State_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kogi State lies in the North Central region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and borders ten other states, the only state in the country to do so. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> word for river, and it is nicknamed the Confluence State because the River Niger and the River Benue meet next to its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokoja">Lokoja</a>. The two rivers meet in the centre of the state and bisect it southward. The state lies within the tropical Guinean forest–savanna mosaic ecoregion, with a rainy season from April to October and a dry season from November to March marked by the harmattan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before colonial rule the area was divided among various states, some tiny and village-based, others part of larger realms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe Kingdom</a> held much of what is now western Kogi until the early 1800s, when the Fulani jihad annexed it and placed the area under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. The eastern half of the modern state was the ancient Igala Kingdom. British expeditions occupied the area in the early twentieth century and made it part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Northern Nigeria Protectorate</a>, whose capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokoja">Lokoja</a> until 1903. According to the account given of Lokoja, the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> was coined there by Flora Shaw, later the wife of Baron Lugard, as she looked out over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> from Mount Patti.</p><p>After independence in 1960 the area was part of the Northern Region until 1967, when the region was split, and then of the Benue, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Kwara states. Kogi State was formed on 27 August 1991 from parts of Benue State, Niger State and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwara-state">Kwara State</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The state is home to the Ebira, Gbagyi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> subgroups such as the Kakanda and Kupa, and Oko in the centre, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a> and Bassa in the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, mainly the Okun, Ogori, Oworo and Magongo, in the west. About 45% of the population is Muslim, about 40% Christian and the remainder follows traditional ethnic religions. Agriculture is the mainstay of the economy, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, cocoa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>, cashews, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a>, yams and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> among the crops. The state has twenty-one local government areas, and its landmarks include Mount Patti, Lord Lugard House and the confluence of the two rivers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kogi State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
